            "reviewBody": "Whenever I make ham this sauce is a must to go with it!!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "1"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "GoldenTongs7892"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe has the right ingredients but wrong proportions. Recipe should be 1/2 cup White Sugar, 1/3 cup white vinegar, 2 tsp. Dry mustard, 2 eggs - well beaten. In a saucepan mix the sugar, vinegar and dry mustard, then whisk in the eggs, then stir over medium heat until thickened. Optional - remove from heat and add 1 Tbsp of butter - whisk to combine.",
            "datePublished": "2023-12-28T18:28:19.605Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Hans-Krishna Von Hagen"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Followed the recipe as written, with only one change: did not have white vinegar, so substituted with same amount of apple cider vinegar. My egg was regular sized, as opposed to a large egg. Used medium heat to until to gentle boil, turned off burner and let cool. My sauce thickness veery nicely, and even more so as it cooled. Very nice, sharp flavour that complimented the ham so very nicely. (Also made an All three of us at the dinner table loved it. A simple recipe to follow for novice cooks, like myself. Will definately make this mustard sauce again. repeat",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-19T07:02:19.743Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Doose"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Easy to make and seems to have the right taste but the recipe calls for 1/3 cup of vinegar. I think 1/4 cup or less would be more than enough, and as mentioned in the previous review, there is way, way, way too much sugar. We used 1/4 cup (60ml) and it was still extremely sweet. I'd suggest you start with 1/8 cup (30ml) and taste as you go. We ended up adding some dribbles of milk &amp; thickened cream to dilute the acidity &amp; that seemed to help a lot but I'd suggest you use the \"less is more\" philosophy to get the most out of this recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2020-06-29T12:32:07.910Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Patricia"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have been making this sauce for 50 years. My family love it- I use half vinegar and half water- if runny I add a bit of cornflower to thicken -",
            "datePublished": "2023-12-13T12:14:35.531Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "framer94"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Loved the idea and made it with half the amount of sugar and still found it too sweet. The consistency was great and it had a pleasing color, but if I ever make it again, I would be very sparing with the sugar, maybe 2 T. at first. Thanks for the recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-17T10:28:37.090Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mikeockurtsalot"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used Apple cider vinegar instead of white and it came out beautiful. Also dumped in a heap of whole grain mustard. Though it was quite runny so I pinched a bit of what I thought was rice flour to thicken it up a bit. Turned out I accidentaly used custard powder instead. Made it weirdly sweet haha. Still worked though. Good recipe..\nJust make sure you don't heat those eggs too much or you'll get scrambled mustard sauce.",
            "datePublished": "2021-10-10T20:37:38.790Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Shelly"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I had to keep stirring to get the consistency I wanted. It will thicken when it cools down. Really good, a little bit tart and sweet enough to contrast.",
            "datePublished": "2026-02-22T23:49:10.053Z"
